CDN logs Pipeline
we want to create longer term cuts of CDN log data primarily aimed at examining traffic on the platform. this process has to be aware of a user's digital fingerprint data that is contained in the raw logs and will need to remove or anonymise where possible.

This is aimed at both long term performance and business analysis of data at a more granular level. for general metrics these can already be calculated and stored in cloudwatch e.g. total traffic. But Cloudwatch is not suitable for examining the paths and parameters being used as it would create too many dimensions/metrics. Eventually this may be replaced by a different tool such as Mixpanel especially for performance metrics.

Input Schema
The below covers the fields coming in, we have split this into required which are the fields we will extract and process in someway where as removed are fields that are in the source data but won't be ingested by the pipeline and won't be read in.

Raw CDN logs

Raw CDN logs are automatically generated by cloudfront and stored in an s3 bucket as well as added to cloudwatch. this pipeline will take them from s3 and turn them into delta lake tables with a structured schema. It can be used on any CDN logs across our applications and services.

Required fields
These fields are needed by the pipeline to continue processing, without them it SHOULD fail

date -> timestamp
    The date (UTC) the event occurred, e.g. 2026-07-15.
    format: UTC string
    Justification: This could serve as primary partition key for the log store. It is also required for any time-series performance reporting and trend analysis
    Verdict:

time -> timestamp
    The time the event occurred in UTC
    Justification: Need to understand the time of day of the request to see when we're getting the most traffic. Enables intra day analysis.
    the time will be limited to an hour to help with is being anonymous.
    Verdict:

time-taken -> time_taken
    Total time in seconds from when CloudFront received the request to when it finished writing the last byte of the response — the primary latency metric.
    Justification: gives us our primary latency statistic with routes, allows us to track latency over a longer period of time to see which routes are driving up latency.
    Processing requirements:  must be rounded down to the hour interval it took place in. this stops the timing be too accurate but can still show us trends over the day
    Verdict:

time-to-first-byte -> time_to_first_byte
    Seconds between CloudFront receiving the request and writing the first byte of the response 
    Justification: useful for distinguishing "slow to start responding" from "slow to finish sending" (e.g., large file transfer vs. slow backend).
    Verdict:

c-ip -> daily_visitor_hash
    The IP address of the viewer that made the request
    Processing requirements: 
    this field must not be in the final result as it can be considered sensitive. see the anonymising method below for how this is used.

cs(User-Agent) -> daily_visitor_hash
    The User-Agent header sent by the viewer
    Processing requirements: this field must not be in the final result as it can be considered sensitive. see the anonymising method below for how this is used.
    Verdict:

cs(Referer) -> referer_domain
    The Referer header — what page/link the request came from, if any.
    Justification: This attribute shows how users arrive at the portal — from LPA sites, GOV.UK, search, or internal navigation. Supports channel analysis and is hugely used in the existing power bi reports.
    Verdict:

cs-method -> http_method
    HTTP method used
    Justification: Separates read traffic from submissions. Essential for API analytics and spotting method-based probing e.g submission volumes vs. browsing (Get, head, Post etc). Was used in first drat of API analytics report.
    Verdict:

cs-uri-stem -> path
    The path portion of the requested URL (e.g., /blog/post-title), URL-encoded.
    Justifcation: Perhaps the most important field for analytics. Drives endpoint-level performance reporting, journey and page analysis, identification of the most-requested documents and API routes, and cache-rule design. Actively used in existing performance report.
    Verdict:

cs-uri-query -> query_params
    The query string portion, if any (e.g., ?ref=twitter), URL-encoded
    Processing requirements: need to transform to a json of key value pairs, this should only happen for a subset of query params identified. This is documented below for each query parameter we want to include.
    Justification: Carries the parameters that define API behaviour: search filters, pagination, layering etc.
    Verdict:

x-host-header -> domain, daily_visitor_hash
    The value of the Host header as CloudFront received it — used to distinguish alternate domain names on the same distribution. In our infrastructure this is the *.planning.data.gov.uk url.
    Verdict:

sc-status -> status_code
    The HTTP status code CloudFront returned to the viewer
    Justification: Underpins the availability and error-rate of service. Evidences uptime and downtime, and just to detect broken links or removed content. Currently in the power bi report, it is used in connection with cs(Referer) to identify what users click that generates errors.
    Verdict:

sc-bytes -> response_bytes
    Number of bytes CloudFront sent to the viewer, including headers (size of the response).
    Justification: Could be needed for cost attribution across services, for bandwidth capacity planning, and for detecting abnormal bulk extraction of documents.
    Verdict:

sc-content-type -> content_type
    The Content-Type header value of the response (e.g., text/html, image/png).
    Justification: Enables analysis by asset class — HTML, JSON API responses, map tiles, PDF and drawing downloads — each of which wull have a distinct performance profile
    Verdict:

x-edge-result-type -> cache_result
    What CloudFront did with the request — Hit, Miss, RefreshHit, Error, Redirect, etc.
    Removed/ignored fields
    This list is known fields in the source data that isn't even read in during the spark job
    Verdict:

c-port
    The port number the viewer's request came from
    Justification: doesn't offer any analytical bonus for a 2 year retention time, useful in raw logs for system monitoring
    Verdict:

cs(Cookie)
    Cookie header content, only present if you've explicitly enabled cookie logging
    Justification: this is likely to be a source of identifiable information and there's no cookies in use that we specifically want to be passed to our applications. this may be revisited in the future but cloudfront should be used to ensure only our cookies are passed through.
    Verdict:

x-forwarded-for
    If the request passed through a proxy before reaching CloudFront, this can contain the original client IP as reported by that proxy
    Justification: no value in keeping for any analysis
    Verdict:

cs(Host)
    The Host header, this is normally the cloudfront host.
    Justification: isn't useful, especially if it were to be modified.
    Verdict:

cs-protocol
    The protocol of the viewer's request — http or https.
    Justification: this should be https for all, might be worth monitoring in live logs but not worth it for analytical purposes
    Verdict:

cs-protocol-version
    The HTTP protocol version — HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2.0, etc.
    Justification: no analytical justification to include this so it's dropped
    Verdict:

cs-bytes
    Number of bytes received by CloudFront from the viewer, including headers (i.e., the size of the request).
    Justification: our application is read only meaning no put requests, we may want to optionally include this based on the application being monitored but initially we can exclude this.
