Methodologies
A set of methodologies that need to be applied in the above pipeline when creating certain fields.

Daily Visitor Hashing
daily_visitor_hash is the anonymous-traffic equivalent of a unique-visitor count and lightweight route-correlation signal, positioned deliberately as an alternative to persistent tracking identifiers (cookies, device IDs, GA-style client IDs), which require consent under GDPR/PECR-style frameworks. This approach follows the same underlying method used by Plausible Analytics, an open-source, privacy-focused analytics platform, and is adopted here — with additional precision-reduction safeguards on top,

How it's calculated
daily_visitor_hash = HMAC-SHA256(
    key = daily_salt,
    message = domain + ip_truncated + ua_bucket
)
inputs:

daily_salt - A cryptographically random value, generated fresh at the start of each processing day and never persisted. See "Salt generation" below.
domain - The site/service the request was made to, scoping the hash so the same visitor produces different hashes on different properties. calculated from x-host-header
ip_truncated - The visitor's IP address with the last octet (IPv4) or final 64 bits (IPv6) zeroed, before hashing. calculated from c-ip see IP truncation below
ua_bucket - The visitor's User-Agent, reduced to {browser family, major version, OS family} before hashing. calculated from cs(User-Agent) see user-agent bucketing below
The raw IP address and raw User-Agent string are never written to disk or persisted anywhere — they exist only transiently, in memory, for the duration of this calculation, and are discarded immediately after.

Salt generation and rotation:

A new daily_salt is generated using a cryptographically secure random number generator (secrets.token_hex(32) — 256 bits of entropy) at the start of each daily batch processing run.
The salt is used only in-memory for the duration of that run and is never written to any persistent store (no database, no secrets manager, no logs). It therefore cannot be recovered or reconstructed after the job completes.
The salt is not derived from the date or any other public/predictable value — it must be independently random, or it would not function as a genuine secret and could be recomputed by anyone.
Because the salt is discarded at the end of each processing run, the same visitor (same IP + UA on the same day) always produces the same hash within that run, but a different, unrelated hash on any other day — even though the underlying IP and User-Agent haven't changed.
Why truncation and bucketing happen before hashing, not just salting:

Salting alone is not sufficient protection for IPv4 addresses. The IPv4 address space (2^32 values) is small enough that, if a salt were ever exposed or reused, an attacker could brute-force every possible IP against it in well under an hour. Truncating the IP before hashing means even a compromised salt would only ever reveal a /24 subnet (up to 256 possible addresses), not a specific host — this is a deliberate defense-in-depth measure on top of salt rotation, not a redundant step.

What this protects against:

Cross-day tracking: because the salt is discarded daily, daily_visitor_hash values cannot be linked across days. A visitor active on two different days will produce two unrelated, uncorrelated hash values.
Brute-force reversal: even within a single day, the combination of IP truncation and a 256-bit random salt makes brute-forcing the original IP/UA from the hash computationally infeasible.
Re-identification via the salt itself: since the salt is never persisted, it cannot be recovered later — even by us — meaning the original IP and User-Agent underlying any given hash are permanently unrecoverable once a processing run completes.
IP truncation.

The last octet of an IPv4 address is zeroed (e.g. 203.0.113.47 → 203.0.113.0), or the final 64 bits of an IPv6 address are zeroed, before the address is used in the hash. This collapses up to 256 possible IPv4 addresses (or the IPv6 equivalent subnet) into a single value prior to hashing, reducing re-identifiability and limiting what a brute-force reversal of the hash could ever reveal to a subnet rather than a specific host.

User-Agent bucketing.

The raw User-Agent string is parsed and reduced to three fields — browser family, browser major version, and OS family (e.g. Chrome, 126, Windows) — discarding minor version numbers, build identifiers, and device-specific details. This removes the long tail of rare, highly specific User-Agent strings that could otherwise make a visitor unusually easy to distinguish, while retaining enough information for browser/OS-share reporting.

